  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ and the given matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}\u2061B=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}\u2061D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}\u2061D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u20611 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061B & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}\u2061D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & 2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & 2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & 2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
